description: Do not use RefreshDatabase or any DB-wiping patterns in unit tests
globs: tests/Unit/**/*.php,tests/**/*Test.php
alwaysApply: false
---

# Unit tests — no database refresh or table clearing

**Never** use traits, helpers, or Artisan commands that wipe, truncate, or rebuild the database schema/data in **`tests/Unit`** (or any test marketed as a unit test).

## Forbidden

- `Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\RefreshDatabase`
- `Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\DatabaseMigrations`
- `Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\LazilyRefreshDatabase`
- `migrate:fresh`, `migrate:refresh`, `db:wipe`, or manual `Schema::drop*` / `TRUNCATE` in test setup
- `Model::truncate()` or deleting all rows from shared tables in `setUp` / `tearDown`

## Required approach

- **Pure unit tests:** exercise services, rules, and helpers with **mocks, stubs, and fakes** — no Eloquent persistence, no HTTP kernel, no `TestCase` database bootstrapping.
- **When persistence is unavoidable:** create only the rows the test needs, assert behavior, then **delete those specific records** (or roll back via a narrow transaction scoped to the test) — never reset the whole database.
- Prefer testing domain logic in isolation (`PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase` or a thin project base) instead of `Tests\TestCase` + factories when no Laravel container is required.

## Examples

```php
// BAD — wipes all tables between tests
class BudgetServiceTest extends TestCase
{
    use RefreshDatabase;
}

// GOOD — mock dependencies, no DB
class BudgetServiceTest extends TestCase
{
    public function test_period_boundary_uses_user_timezone(): void
    {
        $user = Mockery::mock(User::class);
        // ...
    }
}
```

## Feature / integration tests

`tests/Feature` is separate from unit tests. Still **do not** add `RefreshDatabase` or global table clearing there unless the team explicitly changes this rule. Prefer targeted record creation and cleanup.
